ZisK is a zero-knowledge virtual machine designed for practical verifiable computation. This paper has described its four main layers:

- *ISA*: A custom 64-bit virtual instruction set with RISC-V ABI compatibility, accessed through a static transpiler that converts RISC-V ELF binaries. Registers live in the same address space as ordinary RAM, simplifying the memory argument. A rich set of precompile instructions gives single-instruction access to cryptographic primitives (hash functions, elliptic-curve arithmetic) and bulk memory operations (DMA).

- *Arithmetization*: An AIR-based constraint framework where independent AIRs communicate through typed interaction buses. Bus balancing---the core correctness condition---is reduced to a global LogUp sum-check, with each AIR contributing a scalar partial sum as a public output.

- *Continuations*: Execution is split into segments of $2^(22)$ steps. AIR values carry the program counter and last register value across segment boundaries, enforced by a bus protocol that anchors the chain at globally fixed initial and final states. Up to $2^(14)$ segments can be chained, supporting programs of up to $2^(36)$ steps.

- *Proving system*: Individual segment proofs are Goldilocks-field STARKs generated by the PIL2/proofman framework. They are aggregated in a binary tree; the root verifies that all partial sums cancel, confirming global bus balancing and therefore correct execution.

Together, these layers make ZisK a system that a Rust developer can target with minimal changes to their workflow, while offering the prover the flexibility to scale horizontally across distributed hardware.

Future directions include further optimization of the aggregation tree depth, tighter integration of the Poseidon2 and STARK-friendly hash functions into the recursion layer, and extending the precompile set as new cryptographic standards are adopted by blockchain protocols.
